ance and of course what  i've tried to say tonight  is that everything is in flux the whole  philosophy that stands behind the iching  and pythagoreanism and all of that is a  philosophy of eternal change  and another uh well  when you actually study the  philosophy of science what you learn  is two things first of all there's  something called  girdles fear incompleteness  theorem which people are not aware of  but  certainly should be because it bears  centrally on the formation of ideas in  the 20th century  girdle was a mathematician who showed  that in  any formal system including a  mathematical system  it is possible to generate true  statements which are not uh  allowable by the formal laws within the  system  in other words no formal system can  generate all true statements about  itself  well what this means is that  the kind of truth which science has held  as its holy grail  for 2000 years is in fact unattainable  and he showed this very clearly  people who work in fields where girdle's  theorem apply  are aware of this but the general public  is not  so what you now learn when you study the  philosophy of science  is that the most that you can require  of a system of explanation  is that it be consistent unto itself  a theory of explanation which is  consistent unto itself  has arrived at the highest plane  of uh human  knowability there is no meta system  which can look down at five  self-consistent systems  and say well a and b are true  and c d and f are false well  now astrology is a self-consistent  closed system of explanation just  like science is but science will presume  to judge astrology and dismiss  it and we have all gotten into the habit  of thinking that if you have an  idea and you want to know whether it's  true or not  you take it to scientists and lay it  before them the way a cat  lays a rat at its master's feet and then  they  the scientists say yay or nay but  actually scientists are part of a  priesthood  and act to preserve their turf and very  rarely point out  that if a system is self-consistent  that's as far as it can be taken and  this is very important to remember  science  what is not under attack here  is reason i'm very concerned to make the  differentiation between  science and reason because and  and there is a tendency in modern  society and i'm sure there are  speakers who have occupied this chair  who have not  felt the obligation to reason at  all you know i mean they're they're  operating in a realm where  it's whatever you say it is as fast as  you can  think it up and  that's not where i'm coming from so i  think science is  a very very important branch of art  and if we could get the scientists to  admit that the ultimate criteria for  deciding how a person shall  choose among competing explanatory  systems is aesthetic it's a matter of  choice and taste  there is no higher arbiter of meaning  [Music]  you
 

